@@ -967,6 +967,25 @@ to all the metrics scraped by the given `vmagent` instance:
 
 See also [how to shard data among multiple remote storage systems](#sharding-among-remote-storages).
 
+## Limiting the number of targets per job
+
+A misconfigured service discovery source (for example, an overly broad cloud API query, catalog filter,
+or an accidentally removed tag filter) can suddenly return a huge number of targets for a single `job_name`.
+Since `vmagent` scrapes all the discovered targets by default, this can spike CPU and memory usage and degrade
+scraping for every other job running on the same `vmagent` instance.
+
+The `max_scrape_targets` option at [scrape_configs](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs)
+(or the `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag for setting a default across all jobs) protects against this
+by limiting the number of simultaneously scraped targets for a single `job_name`. The limit is applied identically
+regardless of which service discovery mechanism(s) (`kubernetes_sd_configs`, `ec2_sd_configs`, `consul_sd_configs`,
+`static_configs`, etc.) supplied the targets for the job. By default, there is no limit on the number of scrape targets per job,
+so existing setups are unaffected.
+
+When a job's discovered targets exceed the configured limit, `vmagent` keeps scraping only up to the configured number
+of targets for that job, while the excess targets are reported as `DROPPED` (with the reason `too many targets`)
+at the `/targets`, `/api/v1/targets` and `/service-discovery` pages, so operators can quickly notice which job(s)
+are hitting the limit and roughly how many targets are being excluded.
+
 ## High availability
 
 It is possible to run multiple **identically configured** `vmagent` instances or `vmagent`
